# pf-killswitch.conf — leak-proof kill-switch for qbittorrent/sonarr/radarr.
#
# gluetun's whole selling point was: if the tunnel drops, the torrent client
# loses ALL connectivity rather than falling back to your real IP.
#
# Two layers enforce that here. The first is inside the vpn jail itself
# (pf-vpn-jail.conf.tmpl): it only ever forwards the confined jails' traffic
# into the tunnel. This file is the second, appended to the HOST's
# /etc/pf.conf after the jail-NAT rule (pf.conf requires nat before filter
# rules), for the case where the jail's own ruleset is not loaded.
#
# The logic: qbittorrent/sonarr/radarr's default route is the vpn jail. With
# the tunnel up, the vpn jail sends their traffic into wg0 and what reaches
# the host is only encrypted WireGuard packets from the vpn jail's own
# address. With the tunnel down, wg0's routes are gone and the vpn jail would
# hand their packets back to the host to route normally -- unencrypted, still
# carrying the confined jails' source addresses, arriving on the vpn jail's
# OWN leg of the bridge (e0a_<jail>). That arrival is what this blocks.
#
# "in" matters: the same packets legitimately travel OUT of that interface on
# their way from a confined jail to the vpn jail, and a rule with no
# direction drops those too (measured: tunnel up, no traffic at all).
#
# It only works with two sysctls the installer sets, because pf is not shown
# packets on a bridge member by default (measured: rule loaded, 0 packets
# matched, confined jail online with the real address):
#   net.link.bridge.pfil_member=1      filter on member interfaces
#   net.link.bridge.pfil_local_phys=1  ...including packets addressed to the
#                                      host itself, which is what a packet
#                                      sent to the bridge gateway is

block drop in quick on e0a_{{VPN_JAIL_NAME}} from { {{VPN_CONFINED_IPS}} } to ! {{VPN_JAIL_IP}}
